Title:
evil-mhyprot-cli (Mhyprot2.sys vulnerable driver PoC CLI)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This repository provides a command-line PoC that abuses the vulnerable Windows kernel driver **Mhyprot2.sys** to gain arbitrary kernel-mode capabilities from an unprivileged user process.  
- It exposes primitives to **read/write arbitrary kernel memory** and **read/write arbitrary user memory** with ring-0 privileges via the driver’s IOCTL interface.  
- The CLI also includes operational features such as enumerating process modules/threads (including reading kernel `PETHREAD` structures), querying system uptime, and terminating processes via `ZwTerminateProcess` executed in driver context.  
- Admin rights are only needed to start the driver service if it is not already running; once loaded, the tool can be used by a normal user.  
- It is primarily useful for **red teamers, exploit developers, and malware researchers** studying BYOVD-style privilege escalation and kernel memory manipulation.  
- It’s interesting because it demonstrates a practical, reusable interface for turning a third-party signed/installed driver into a ring-0 capability set.

Technical Focus:
- BYOVD (Bring Your Own Vulnerable Driver) abuse
- Arbitrary kernel memory read/write primitives via IOCTL
- User↔kernel memory access from ring-0 context
- Kernel object/structure inspection (e.g., `PETHREAD`)
- Process/thread/module enumeration from kernel context
- Kernel-assisted process termination (`ZwTerminateProcess`)

Use Cases:
- Local privilege escalation and post-exploitation via vulnerable driver abuse
- Building kernel read/write primitives for token stealing or EDR tampering research
- Kernel memory forensics/inspection in lab environments
- Demonstrating risk of preinstalled/left-behind vulnerable drivers on endpoints
- Developing detections around suspicious IOCTL usage and driver service presence

Keywords:
Mhyprot2.sys, mhyprot, BYOVD, vulnerable driver, Windows kernel, ring-0, IOCTL, arbitrary read/write, kernel memory, user memory, PETHREAD, ZwTerminateProcess, process enumeration, thread enumeration, driver service, privilege escalation, Windows x64, kernel exploit, libmhyprot